  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1206 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nMain forecast concern continues to be the upper trough and Polo\nremnants moving into the Southern Plains/Rockies for mid week.\nGeneral idea hasn't changes, with the remnants of Polo moving \ninto Mexico and towards New Mexico sometime Tuesday Night. A \ndeeper upper trough will be moving into Arizona at that time, \npicking up the Polo moisture and wrapping it into the upper \nsystem. This upper system will drift across the Panhandle and \ntowards Kansas, but it may be well into Thursday or early Friday \nbefore that happens. \n\nModels have their differences with regards to the details however,\nwith the GFS a little farther east and the ECMWF and Canadian a \nlittle farther west with the position of the upper low. This has \na major impact for West Central Texas with regards to a possible \ndry slot moving into the area that the GFS shows, cutting precip \noff a lot faster than many of the other global models and pushing \nmuch of the rain into Central Texas. This split in the operational\nmodels shows up in the ensembles as well, with some showing heavy\nrain across West Central Texas and others bringing the dry slot\nthrough and shifting the heavy rain east. With this in mind, will\nstick closer to the model blends for now. Thus will go with a \nlittle wetter forecast than the operational GFS shows and closer \nto the blends. There are also differences with regards to timing \nof a few shortwaves as well, which has an impact on timing of any \nrounds of convection.\n\nNow...possible impacts. GFS has the dry slot and minimizes heavy\nrainfall potential. However, if the wetter ensembles are correct,\nmoisture will remain high and the atmosphere primed. Precipitable\nwater values would be well above 2 inches across the area, which \nwould be very high for this late in the summer/early fall. In some\nplaces this may be near 99% of historical values so heavy \nrainfall potential is a real concern. Looking at some of the \nstatistical-based info available, mean rainfall totals off the \nLREF across West Central Texas are generally in the 2-3 inch \nrange, ranging from a 10% lower case of around 1/2 inch to a 90% \nof 3-4 inches. WPC has already placed part of the area in the \nslight risk for Excessive Rainfall, and as the details become a \nlittle more dialed in, would not surprise me if this gets \nexpanded. \n\nFinally, as the upper low finally shifts northeast of the area, a\nstronger cold front makes its way through the area. After a\ncontinuation of well above normal highs through Tuesday, highs by\nThursday and Friday could stay in the 70s in some area, or cooler\nif clouds and rain persist. Morning lows may well fall into the\n50s in some locations by Friday morning.",
